/* Baker School of Aeronautics (2022) - Airframe oral question banks. */
window.BAKER_AIRFRAME_BANKS=[{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-a","letter":"A","title":"A. Wood Structures","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-A-001","question":"What species of wood is used as a standard for strength properties of other species of wood used in aircraft structures?","idealAnswer":"Spruce is used as the standard of which all woods are compared.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-002","question":"Is northern white pine an acceptable substitute for spruce?","idealAnswer":"Yes, but only if the pine is a larger size to make up for its lower strength.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-003","question":"Are knots allowed in aircraft wood?","idealAnswer":"Knots are allowed if within limitations. Factors that determine if knots are acceptable include the type of knot, location, spacing, and number of knots.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-004","question":"What type of wood quality must be used in the repair of aircraft structures?","idealAnswer":"The wood must be of aircraft quality and equivalent to its original strength or greater.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-005","question":"What is the difference between laminated wood and plywood?","idealAnswer":"iy Laminated wood and plywood consist of two or more layers of wood which have been glued ; together. Laminated wood has the grain of all layers approximately parallel. The grain of plywood ; runs 90° with the adjacent ply.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-006","question":"What are two types of glue used for aircraft wood structure repair?","idealAnswer":"They are casein and synthetic resin glue. The resin glues that are recommended for wooden aircraft applications are the resorcinol formaldehyde type glues.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-007","question":"What method is used to prepare plywood for bending?","idealAnswer":"The wood is soaked in hot water, and then placed in a form until completely dry.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-008","question":"What are the results of using insufficient gluing pressure when making a wood joint?","idealAnswer":"The results will normally be thick glue lines, which indicate a weak joint.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-009","question":"What is the recommended clamping pressure for a glued joint?","idealAnswer":"125 to 150 psi.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-010","question":"How long should a wood glue joint be kept under pressure before removing the clamps?","idealAnswer":"The pressure time will vary depending on the temperature of the curing room. The curing temperatures are from 70°F and up. Higher temperatures will cause the glue to set faster. To be certain that a glue joint is satisfactory, it is recommended that it remain under pressure at about 85°F for a period of at least seven hours. Full joint strength will develop only after conditioning for at least two days.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-011","question":"What are some of the various methods used to apply pressure to joints in aircraft gluing operations?","idealAnswer":"Brads, nails, screws, clamps, or hydraulic and electric power presses.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-012","question":"Why does the strength of a scarf joint depend upon the accuracy of the two beveled surfaces?","idealAnswer":"Because an inaccurate bevel will reduce the amount of effective glue area.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-013","question":"Why is sanding unacceptable when preparing a joint for splicing?","idealAnswer":"Because the dust created from sanding will clog the pores of the wood, preventing proper penetration of glue. Suan Lp eal -_ eee Piva bad s","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-014","question":"What type of patch is preferred for repair of stressed plywood skin and why?","idealAnswer":"C A properly prepared and inserted scarf patch is the best repair for plywood, and is preferred for most skin repairs. It differs from a splayed patch in that the edges are scarfed to a 12 to 1 slope ) instead of a 5 to 1 slope, and it uses reinforcements under the patch where the glue joints occur. bh)","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-015","question":"Are mineral streaks acceptable in aircraft wood?","idealAnswer":"Yes, providing careful inspection fails to reveal any decay.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-016","question":"How do you inspect for decay/rot in wood?","idealAnswer":"Lo Visual (dark discoloration or gray stains), Odor (musty or moldy), Tapping (sharp solid report), Probe pa (Sharp metal tool), Prying (bonded joints) we ‘ \"","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-017","question":"How can the moisture content of wood be determined?","idealAnswer":"a One way is to use a moisture meter. However, the most accurate method is to take a sample of the y wood, weigh it, dry it in an oven at a temperature of 100°C to 105°C, reweigh it, and then compute the moisture content by using the following formula. si W1-W2 44 w2 « wi the weight of the wood sample before drying. w2 = the weight of the wood sample after drying. '","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-018","question":"What area of the wood spar may not be spliced?","idealAnswer":"€ It may not be spliced under wing attachment fittings, landing gear fittings, engine mount fittings, or i lift-and-interplane strut fittings.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-019","question":"What type of joint is generally used in splicing structural members in aircraft?","idealAnswer":"A scarf joint.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-020","question":"What is compression wood and why is it not acceptable for aircraft structures?","idealAnswer":"Compression wood is wood that is characterized by high specific gravity and has the appearance of ° an excessive growth of summer wood (hard, non-porous). This defect is difficult to recognize and is . very detrimental to strength. Reject all material containing compression wood. a","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-021","question":"What are three forms of wood?","idealAnswer":"Solid, Ply, and Laminate. ry","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-022","question":"What are two forms of wood that are acceptable for the repair of wooden aircraft skin?","idealAnswer":"The plywood and laminated woods are preferred for this application.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-023","question":"What would be the greatest number of splices placed into one spar?","idealAnswer":"Two. \"0","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-A-024","question":"What is the largest hole diameter that can be repaired in a plywood wing skin when using a fabric patch?","idealAnswer":"1 Inch","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-b","letter":"B","title":"B. Aircraft Covering","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-B-001","question":"What determines the required quality and strength of fabric that is to be used for covering aircraft?","idealAnswer":"1 The required strength and quality of aircraft fabric is determined by the pounds per square foot of iy wing loading (PSF), and the never-exceed airspeed of the aircraft (VNE).","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-002","question":"When are anti-tear strips recommended and where are they used?","idealAnswer":"Anti-tear strips are installed over the ribs, longerons, and stringers but are under the reinforcing tape before the fabric is installed. They are recommended for all areas in the slipstream.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-003","question":"What is the maximum permissible deterioration of aircraft fabric before it is considered unairworthy?","idealAnswer":"Fabric covering is considered unairworthy when it deteriorates more than 30 percent from its original required tensile strength.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-004","question":"What should be the strength of the fabric used for re-covering an aircraft?","idealAnswer":"All fabric, surface tape, reinforcing tape, thread and lacing cord used to re-cover or repair an aircraft covering should be of high-grade aircraft textile material. The material must be at least as good quality and of equivalent strength as those originally used by the aircraft manufacturer.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-005","question":"What is the purpose and location of drain grommets in aircraft fabric?","idealAnswer":"} They allow the condensed moisture to leave the aircraft as well as furnish ventilation. Drain i grommets are located on the underside of airfoils at the trailing edge as close to the rib as i practical, and at the lowest point along the center of the underside of each fuselage bay.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-006","question":"How are drain grommets installed in aircraft fabric?","idealAnswer":"Brass grommets should be mounted on fabric patches and then doped to the covering. Plastic grommets are doped directly to the covering. Both types are installed with a second coat of dope, and the drain holes are opened by cutting out the fabric in the center of the grommet.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-007","question":"What is the purpose of reinforcing tape?","idealAnswer":"Reinforcing tape is used under all stitching and prevents the stitching | cord from tearing through the fabric.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-008","question":"What type of fabric is not affected by moisture or mildew?","idealAnswer":"Fiberglass fabric is not affected by moisture, mildew, | chemicals, and most acids.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-009","question":"What methods are used to check the strength of doped fabric?","idealAnswer":"A fabric punch test (Maul Test) will provide a , general indication of the strength of the fabric, but if a punch tester indicates that the fabric strength is marginal, a laboratory test should be performed to determine the actual strength of the fabric.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-010","question":"What type of machine-sewed seems are used in aircraft covering?","idealAnswer":"Plain overlap seams, folded-fell seams, and French-fell seams. v","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-011","question":"What should be done with structure that will come in contact with doped fabric?","idealAnswer":"Treat all parts of the structure that will come in contact with doped fabric with a protective coating such as aluminum foil, dope proof paint, or cellulose tape.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-012","question":"If the original rib stitch spacing cannot be determined when re-covering an aircraft, where can you find the rib stitch spacing that should be used?","idealAnswer":"Use the rib stitch spacing chart in the AC 43.13-1B.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-013","question":"What is the standard tie-off knot used during rib stitching?","idealAnswer":"A modified seine knot is used to tie off all stitches except the starting stitch.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-014","question":"What do you call the edge of cloth, tape, or webbing that has been woven to prevent raveling?","idealAnswer":"Selvage edge. (This question has been known to be reversed) The answer is the question. 16. The type of fabric used for covering aircraft are organic and synthetic. Name two organic and two synthetic types. The organic fibers are cotton and linen. The synthetic fibers include fiber glass and heat shrinkable synthetic fibers such as nylon and Dacron.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-015","question":"When covering aircraft, where is surface tape used?","idealAnswer":"Sewed seams, lapped edges, and all stitching is covered with surface tape. The surface tape has a pinked edge to prevent unravelling during the installation process.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-016","question":"How are wrinkles removed from cotton fabric?","idealAnswer":"By wetting the wrinkles and allowing it to dry.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-017","question":"When is finishing tape applied?","idealAnswer":"After the second coat of dope is dry, and the nap has been sanded away.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-018","question":"How wide should surface tape be when used to cover the trailing edge of an aircraft wing?","idealAnswer":"Three inches.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-B-019","question":"What is the recommended type of repair for an L-shaped tear when the legs are more than 14 inches long?","idealAnswer":"A doped-on patch, if the never-exceed velocity Is less than 150 mph.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-c","letter":"C","title":"C. Aircraft Finishes","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-C-001","question":"What primer is used when maximum protection of the metal is desired?","idealAnswer":"Epoxy.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-002","question":"What type of test can be performed to identify existing paint finishes?","idealAnswer":"Apply a coat of engine oil to a small area. Nitrocellulose will soften in a few minutes, but acrylic and epoxy will show no effects. If not identified, next wipe down a small area with MEK. MEK will pick up pigment from an acrylic finish but will not affect an epoxy coating.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-003","question":"What causes blushing when applying dope or lacquer, and how can it be prevented?","idealAnswer":"When doping is accomplished under humid conditions, blushing is possible. Rapid evaporation of thinners and solvents lower the temperature of the surface of the wet dope causing condensation of moisture from the air. The moisture causes a milky white or cloudy appearance known as blush. The condition may be eliminated by heating the room to decrease humidity, or by using a blush-retarding thinner to increase the drying time. Blushing is also caused by moisture in the air supply, drafts, or change in temperature.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-004","question":"What is the purpose of using fungicidal dope when finishing aircraft fabric?","idealAnswer":"To prevent micro-biological deterioration (rotting) of the fabric.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-005","question":"What are the identification marking requirements for United States registered fixed wing aircratt?","idealAnswer":"The Roman capitol letter \"N\" followed by the registration number must be displayed on both sides of the fuselage, or on hoth sides of the vertical tail surfaces. The height of the characters must be equal in size and be at least 12 inches, and the characters must be 2/3 as wide as they are high. The exceptions to the width rule are the number \"1\", which must be 1/6 as wide as it is high, and the letters \"M\" and \"W\", which may be as wide as they are high. Characters must be formed by solid lines 1/6 as thick as they are high, and the spacing between each character may not be less than 1/4 of the character width.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-006","question":"What should the color of registration markings be?","idealAnswer":"The color of markings should contrast their background.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-007","question":"What are two types of dope used for aircraft finishes?","idealAnswer":"They are nitrate and butyrate.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-008","question":"What is the advantage of butyrate over nitrate?","idealAnswer":"Less flammable.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-009","question":"What would happen if dope was ever used over paint or enamel?","idealAnswer":"It would have the tendency to lift and remove such materials.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-010","question":"What is applied to metallic surfaces as a common resistant covering before the application of enamel or lacquer?","idealAnswer":"Zinc chromate primer is normally used for this purpose.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-011","question":"What can be done to the surface of components to prevent filiform corrosion?","idealAnswer":"Ensure that the primer is completely cured. | CERES \"te","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-012","question":"What are the most common methods of applying aircraft finishes?","idealAnswer":"They are dipping, brushing, or spraying.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-013","question":"What type of paint may be used over any paint system that is in good condition?","idealAnswer":"Epoxy topcoats will adhere to all paint systems that are in good condition and may be used as a general touchup, including touchup of defects in baked enamel coatings.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-014","question":"What normally causes runs and sags in a spray application of a paint finish?","idealAnswer":"Holding the gun too long in one place or holding the gun too close can cause too much paint to be applied in one area.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-015","question":"What causes an \"orange peel\" or \"pebble\" finish?","idealAnswer":"v Incorrect spray gun setting, incorrect air pressure, incorrect paint viscosity, and holding the gun too far from the work.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-016","question":"What causes fisheyes in paint?","idealAnswer":"Surface contamination.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-017","question":"What is used to thin chromate primer?","idealAnswer":"Toluene.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-018","question":"Which federal regulation prescribes the size and location requirements for aircraft identification numbers?","idealAnswer":"FAR Part 45.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-019","question":"What type of materials should be thoroughly protected when using paint remover?","idealAnswer":"Synthetic rubber surfaces, aircraft tires, fabric, and acrylics must be protected against possible © contact with paint remover.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-020","question":"What instructions should be followed when covering an aircraft with materials specified in an STC?","idealAnswer":". The instructions are included in the STC.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-021","question":"What is the principal cause of filiform corrosion?","idealAnswer":"Uncured primer.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-022","question":"What is zinc chromate used for?","idealAnswer":"Primer for bare metal. ,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-023","question":"What does paint blushing look like?","idealAnswer":"Dull and hazy surface finish.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-024","question":"What causes paint dust?","idealAnswer":"Poor surface prep and overspray.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-C-025","question":"What types of paint are generally used on aircraft?","idealAnswer":"Epoxy and enamel. ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-d","letter":"D","title":"D. Sheet Metal and Non-Metallic Structures","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-D-001","question":"What is the grip length of a rivet?","idealAnswer":"The grip length is the combined thickness of the materials to be joined by the rivet.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-002","question":"Where are special rivets, such as Huck or Cherry lock rivets used?","idealAnswer":"They are used in places where access to both sides of the riveted structure is impossible, or where limited space will not permit the use of a bucking bar. These rivets require special tools, installation, and removal procedures. They are commonly called blind rivets.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-003","question":"What is bonded honeycomb (sandwich) construction?","idealAnswer":"It is a laminar construction consisting of a combination of alternating dissimilar materials, assembled and fixed in relation to each other so that the properties of each can be used to attain specific structural advantages for the whole assembly while reducing weight.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-004","question":"What type of materials are used in honeycomb construction?","idealAnswer":"Honeycomb construction may employ stainless steel, titanium, magnesium, plywood, resin impregnated paper, E- glass, nylon in various combinations.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-005","question":"Where are sandwich construction assemblies used?","idealAnswer":"They are used for such areas as bulkheads, control surfaces, fuselage panels, wing panels, radomes, empennage skins, or shear webs.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-006","question":"What procedures should be used to prevent damage to the hole when drilling though Plexiglas?","idealAnswer":", The Plexiglas should be backed with wood and the feed slowed as the drill point breaks through the underside of the sheet. A drill bit that is to be used for this purpose should be modified to a 60° tip angle, the cutting edge to a zero rake angle, and the back lip clearance angle increased to 12-15 degrees.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-007","question":"What calculations must be made when bending sheet metal?","idealAnswer":"Setback and bend allowance must be determined. Set back depends on two factors: (1) radius, (2) thickness. Bend allowance depends on four factors: (1) the degree of bend, (2) the radius of the bend, (3) the thickness of the metal, and (4) the type of metal used. The amount of material required for the bend must be determined to assure that the final dimensions will be correct. Bending a strip of metal compresses the materiel on the inside of the curve, and stretches the material on the outside of the curve. However, in the approximate center between these two extremes is a space that is neither stretched nor compressed which is called the neutral axis. When making a bend to exact dimensions, the length of the neutral line must be calculated so enough material can be allowed for the bend.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-008","question":"What factors are used to determine setback?","idealAnswer":"The radius of the bend and the thickness of the material. Setback= | Radius+ Thickness.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-009","question":"When bending sheet metal, how should the grain be in relation to the bend?","idealAnswer":"The grain should be perpendicular to | the bend.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-010","question":"What is a bend tangent line?","idealAnswer":"The line at which the metal starts to bend. i","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-011","question":"What is a joggle?","idealAnswer":"A joggle is an offset near the edge of a piece of sheet metal to allow clearance for a sheet or extrusion.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-012","question":"What are several methods used in forming sheet metal?","idealAnswer":"Folding, bumping, crimping, shrinking, and stretching.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-013","question":"What kind of metal forming is done by bumping?","idealAnswer":"Compound curves.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-014","question":"What is crazing and how does it occur?","idealAnswer":"Hairline cracks in the surface of plastic. A form of heat damage caused by uneven heating, one side expanding more than the other causing excessive stress. Subjecting plastic to large stresses and exposure to harmful solvents will cause crazing. 15. Briefly describe the anodizing process and what purpose it serves. 2 Aluminum alloys are placed in an electrolytic bath which causes a thin film of hydroxide to form on $ the surface of the aluminum. This anodized coating not only provides excellent resistance to . corrosion, but it is also an excellent bond for paint.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-015","question":"What are the proper dimensions for the shop head of a rivet?","idealAnswer":"1.5 times the diameter in width and .5 times the diameter in height after bucking. 1 17. When inspecting Plexiglas, what should you look for? Crazing and discoloration.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-016","question":"What causes honeycomb structure to delaminate?","idealAnswer":"Moisture.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-017","question":"What are the acceptable repair methods for bonded honeycomb structure that has been damaged?","idealAnswer":": There are two acceptable methods of repair currently being used on damaged skin and core - materials of bonded honeycomb structures. One is the potted repair method. The other is the laminated glass fabric cloth overlay which is applied to honeycomb damage which exceeds the . repair limitations of the potted compound method.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-018","question":"What size drill should be used for the common shank rivet diameters of 3/32 inch, 4/32 inch, and 5/32 inch?","idealAnswer":". For a 3/32 rivet, a number 40. For a 4/32 rivet, a number 30. For a 5/32 rivet, a number 21.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-019","question":"What is used for hole filling in a bonded honeycomb repair?","idealAnswer":"Potting compound.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-020","question":"What is a lightening hole?","idealAnswer":"Lightening holes are cut into rib sections, fuselage frames, and other structural parts to decrease weight. Flanges are pressed around the holes for strength.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-021","question":"What special precautions should be observed when repairing a radome?","idealAnswer":"The repair must retain its strength and electrical transparency. °","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-022","question":"What is a warp line?","idealAnswer":"A highlighted strand in the fabric used for ply orientation.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-023","question":"What does \"pot-life\" mean?","idealAnswer":"The length of time a resin will remain workable after the catalyst has been added.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-024","question":"What is a Barcol hardness tester?","idealAnswer":"P A device that pushes a spike into material such as composites. The depth of penetration can be measured to determine if resins in the composite have cured.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-D-025","question":"What is done to honeycomb before repairing?","idealAnswer":"Remove damaged honeycomb and either replace with potting compound or use a honeycomb replacement plug. ae ia","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-e","letter":"E","title":"E. Welding","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-E-001","question":"What type of weld joint must be used to weld magnesium?","idealAnswer":"Only butt welds are used to weld magnesium. This is to prevent the possibility of trapping contaminates.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-002","question":"Why must all flux be removed from the metal after welding magnesium?","idealAnswer":"Any flux left on the metal will result in severe corrosion.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-003","question":"What must he dane to successfully weld titanium?","idealAnswer":"The weld zone must be shielded with an inert gas such as Helium or Argon.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-004","question":"What is gas shielded arc welding?","idealAnswer":"A gas is used as a covering shield around the arc to prevent the atmosphere from contaminating the weld.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-005","question":"What are the advantages of gas-shielded arc welding?","idealAnswer":"It results in a stronger, more ductile, and more corrosion resistant weld.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-006","question":"What shielding gasses are used for gas tungsten arc (TIG) welding?","idealAnswer":"Helium and argon.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-007","question":"What is an advantage of electric arc welding over gas welding?","idealAnswer":"It causes less buckling and warping of the welded material.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-008","question":"What determines the amount of heat that will be applied to the work when welding by the oxyacetylene . method?","idealAnswer":"The torch tip's orifice size or diameter.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-009","question":"What is meant by a soft flame?","idealAnswer":"A soft flame is when the torch is adjusted so the flamc makes no noise and doesn't push the weld puddle.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-010","question":"When gas welding, what should be turned off first?","idealAnswer":"First shut off the acetylene, then the oxygen source. \"A comes before O\"","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-011","question":"What will be the result of using a torch tip that is too large or too small?","idealAnswer":"If the torch tip is too large, the heat will be too great, and holes may be burned in the metal. If the torch tip is too small, the heat provided may be insufficient to produce penetration to the proper depth causing a low strength weld.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-012","question":"How do you determine the correct size filler rod to be used in welding?","idealAnswer":". The diameter of the rod to be used is governed by the thickness of the metals being joined. Welding i rods are made in standard 36-inch lengths, and in diameters from 1/16 to 3/8 inch. If the rod is too small, it will not conduct heat away from the puddle rapidly enough, and a burned weld will result. A filler rod that is too large will cool the puddle too quickly and not allow the proper penetration.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-013","question":"What is the function of flux when used in soldering?","idealAnswer":"Flux erodes corrosion from the surface of the metal when it is heated so the solder can form a strong i bond.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-014","question":"Why must acid-core solder never be used on electrical wire?","idealAnswer":"; Any acid flux that is left over will cause severe corrosion to the wire.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-015","question":"What is brazing?","idealAnswer":"Brazing is a metal joining process in which the bonding material is a non-ferrous metal with a melting point lower than that of the metals being used. It includes silver soldering, copper brazing, and aluminum brazing. It can be used to join metals that are damaged by high heat.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-016","question":"What is one method of controlling expansion when welding a joint?","idealAnswer":"By tack welding at intervals along the joint.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-017","question":"Why is it especially desirable to use a soft flame when welding aluminum?","idealAnswer":"To avoid blowing holes in the metal ef when the puddle is formed.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-018","question":"What fuel is used for gas welding aluminum?","idealAnswer":"Oxygen and hydrogen.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-019","question":"What is the purpose of using flux when welding aluminum?","idealAnswer":"Aluminum and its alloys combine with air and form oxides very rapidly, and oxides form doubly fast . if the metal is hot. Aluminum welding flux is designed to remove the aluminum oxide by chemically : combining with it. Aluminum fluxes dissolve below the surface of the puddle and float the oxides to the surface where they can be removed. Y","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-020","question":"What is the maximum safe pressure for acetylene gas when welding?","idealAnswer":"° When acetylene gas is compressed in a container to a pressure greater than 15 psi, it becomes * dangerously unstable. At 29.4 psi, acetylene is self-explosive and only a slight shock can cause it to ignite.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-021","question":"How can dents at a steel tube cluster joint be repaired?","idealAnswer":"Weld a specially formed steel patch over the dented area and surrounding tubes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-022","question":"What type of flame should be used when silver soldering?","idealAnswer":"The flame should be neutral.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-023","question":"What are the procedures for preparing a metal fuel tank for welding?","idealAnswer":"The interior of the tank should be washed with hot water and detergent, and then steamed for a minimum of thirty minutes. This procedure will vaporize and remove any residual fuel in the tank. Then fill the tank with CO2 gas from a fire extinguisher and seal the tank except for a small vent hole.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-024","question":"When splicing tubing by the inner sleeve method, what method can be used to insert a tight-fitting inner sleeve into the replacement tube?","idealAnswer":"The inner sleeve can be chilled with dry ice or in cold water, and the outer sleeve can be heated. This will allow for a tighter fit repair.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-E-025","question":"Why is flux used on silver solder?","idealAnswer":"It cleans the base metal of oxide to ensure a good bond.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-f","letter":"F","title":"F. Assembly and Rigging","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-F-001","question":"What are three types ot commonly used flight control systems?","idealAnswer":"They are the cable, the push-pull tube, and the torque tube systems. The cable system is the most widely used because deflections of the structure to which it is attached do not affect its operation.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-002","question":"What is used in large metal aircraft to keep control cable tension within acceptable limits?","idealAnswer":"A cable tension regulator. 3. Whal is a fairlead? It is a cable guide that slightly deflects straight run cables around structural features such as bulkheads or lightning holes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-003","question":"How much is cable deflection is acceptable due to a fairlead?","idealAnswer":"3°","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-004","question":"Where does failure of control cables occur most frequently?","idealAnswer":"Breakage of wire strands occur most frequently where cables pass over pulleys and through fairleads.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-005","question":"Which flight control surfaces are considered the primary group?","idealAnswer":"The elevators, the ailerons, and the rudder.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-006","question":"Which flight controls are considered the secondary group?","idealAnswer":"The trim tabs, spring tabs, wing flaps, servo tabs, and the anti-servo tabs. They do not alone cause rotation of the aircraft about its axes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-007","question":"Which flight controls are in the auxiliary group?","idealAnswer":"Think of these as HIGH LIFT devices for slow speed flight. Trailing Edge Flaps, Slats, Slots, Speed Brakes etc.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-008","question":"How do wing flaps affect landing speed and approach angle of an aircraft?","idealAnswer":"The use of flaps increases the camber of a wing and thercforc the lift of the wing, making it possible for the speed of the aircraft to be decreased without decreasing the lift produced by the wing. This permits a steeper glided angle and lower speed to be obtained for the landing approach.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-009","question":"What is a balance tab?","idealAnswer":"It is an auxiliary control with fixed linkage that is designed in such a way that when the | primary control surface is moved the tab moves In the opposite direction. Aerodynamic t forces (back pressure) acting on the tab assists the pilot in moving the control surface.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-010","question":"What is a trim tab?","idealAnswer":"It is an auxiliary control attached to the trailing edge of the primary control surface that is positioned by movement of a cockpit control to reduce the workload on the pilot in maintaining a desired flight attitude.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-011","question":"What is the purpose of the collective pitch control in a helicopter?","idealAnswer":"It is used to change the pitch of the main rotor blades therefore increasing or decreasing lift.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-012","question":"Why is it important for the blades of a helicopter rotor system to be tracked?","idealAnswer":"To reduce vertical vibrations.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-013","question":"What is the most efficient way to check a helicopter rotor for dynamic balance?","idealAnswer":"The use of a balance/analyzer computer.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-014","question":"What unit on a helicopter is used to compensate for torque created by the main rotor?","idealAnswer":"The tail rotor. a) ™~","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-015","question":"What control operates the helicopter tail rotor?","idealAnswer":"The anti-torque pedals.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-016","question":"What are the three axes of an aircraft, and which control surfaces cause the aircraft to move about each axis?","idealAnswer":"ba) They are the longitudinal, the lateral, and the vertical. The longitudinal axis extends from nose to tail + and uses ailerons for roll. The lateral axis extends from wingtip to wingtip using the elevators for * pitch. And the vertical axis passes through the center of the airframe from top to bottom, and uses the rudder for yaw. a 2 18. When installing an aircraft bolt, what precaution would you take regarding the position of the bolt? a Where possible, the bolt head should be positioned on top when a bolt is installed vertically, and the J bolt head should be forward when it is installed horizontally. In these positions the bolt is less likely a to slide out if the locking device fails.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-017","question":"How is the propeller torque corrected for during assembly and rigging in some single engine aircraft?","idealAnswer":"e Some aircraft have the leading edge of the vertical stabilizer offset to the longitudinal center line to fad counteract engine torque.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-018","question":"What reference publications should be used to rig aircraft, including the control system?","idealAnswer":"You should refer to the Aircraft Specifications or Type Certificate Data Sheets issued by the FAA, and ® the Maintenance Manual issued by the manufacturer. ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-019","question":"What are vortex generators?","idealAnswer":"Small, low-aspect-ratio airfoils mounted on the upper surface of wings. Their function is to dampen shock-induced separation of the airstream which results in a stall. .","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-020","question":"What is a servo tab?","idealAnswer":"A cockpit controlled adjustable tab attached to the trailing edge of a primary control surface. The tab moves opposite the direction of the control surface producing an aerodynamic force that moves ® the primary. )","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-021","question":"What factors are used to determine the proper cable tension?","idealAnswer":"The size of the cable and the outside temperature.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-022","question":"Why do control surfaces need to be statically balanced?","idealAnswer":"Because an out of balance control surface can cause a severe flutter.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-023","question":"What is a spring tab?","idealAnswer":"A flight control that uses a spring to assist control surface movement at high speeds.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-F-024","question":"What tool can be used to measure control surface travel?","idealAnswer":"A protractor. © e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-g","letter":"G","title":"G. Airframe Inspection","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-G-001","question":"What needs to be done to prepare an aircraft for an annual or 100-hour inspection?","idealAnswer":"Remove or open all necessary inspection plates, access doors, fairings, and cowlings. Then thoroughly clean the aircraft and the engine.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-G-002","question":"What is the difference between an annual and 100 -hour inspection?","idealAnswer":"Except for the difference in time between inspections and the person who can sign it off, there is no difference between the annual and the 100-hour inspection. They are identical in scope and detail.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-G-003","question":"Who has the authority to sign off and return to service a 100-hour inspection?","idealAnswer":"An appropriately rated mechanic may return an aircraft to service after a 100-hour inspection.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-G-004","question":"What are the operating conditions which make the 100-hour inspection mandatory?","idealAnswer":"The 100-hour inspection is required for aircraft that carry persons for hire or is used to give flight instruction for hire.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-G-005","question":"Where can you find a checklist for the 100-hour inspection?","idealAnswer":"In FAR Part 43, Appendix D.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-G-006","question":"Where would you find the recommended statement for recording the approval or disapproval for return to service of an aircraft after a 100-hour inspection?","idealAnswer":"In FAR Part 43,11.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-G-007","question":"Who can approve an aircraft for return to service after an annual inspection?","idealAnswer":"An A&P mechanic who holds an Inspection Authorization (IA) certificate.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-G-008","question":"Who can approve an aircraft for return to service after a progressive inspection has been performed?","idealAnswer":"Acertified mechanic holding an Inspection Authorization, the aircraft manufacturer, or a certified repair station.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-G-009","question":"Does an aircraft being operated under a progressive inspection program require a 100-hour inspection if it is used to give flight instruction for hire?","idealAnswer":"No.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-G-010","question":"What is the maximum time that a 100-hour inspection may be extended?","idealAnswer":"Not more than 10 hours. The 10 hours may only be used to ferry the aircraft to the next inspection site. ATE Naa LL AP NPE NE TEE EL PP PS OPT BE Ee Section Ill - Airframe Systems and Components Oral Questions Section Ill - Airframe System and Components (10 Subjects) Aircraft Landing Gear Systems [Subject K | _52 Hydraulic and Pneumatic Power Systems 54 F Cabin Atmosphere Control Systems Subject M 56 , | Aircraft Instrument Systems [SubjectN | 58 | + Communication and Navigation Systems . Aircraft Fuel Systems [SubjectP | 62 | ‘ Aircraft Electrical Systems 64 &% Position and Warning Systems [SubjectR | 66 | Ice and Rain Control Systems","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-k","letter":"K","title":"K. Aircraft Landing Gear Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-K-001","question":"What are two sources of power used to extend and retract landing gear?","idealAnswer":"They are electrical and hydraulic.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-002","question":"When should landing gear retraction checks be accomplished?","idealAnswer":"During annual and other type inspections, after replacing landing gear components, and after any hard landing.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-003","question":"What is used to inflate an oleo type landing gear shock strut?","idealAnswer":"Inflate the strut with a high-pressure source of dry air or nitrogen.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-004","question":"Where would you look to find proper tire inflation information?","idealAnswer":"When inflating aircraft tires, the amount of pressure is determined by such factors as tire size, outside air temperature, and the gross weight of the aircraft. Specific tire inflation information may be found in the Operation Manual and the Aircraft Maintenance Manual in special tire inflation pressure charts based on gross weight.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-005","question":"What is the purpose of a hydraulic shimmy damper?","idealAnswer":"It is a unit designed to prevent nose wheel vibrations or \"shimmy\" during taxiing, landing, or takeoff.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-006","question":"What type of care should be given to landing gear shock struts?","idealAnswer":"They should be inspected regularly for leakage of fluid and for proper extension. The exposed portion of the strut piston should be wiped clean daily and inspected for scoring or corrosion.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-007","question":"What is the effect of under inflated tires?","idealAnswer":"Under inflated tires are more likely to creep or slip on the wheel when landing or when brakes are applied. Under inflation also causes rapid, uneven wear at or near the edges of the tread.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-008","question":"What is the purpose of the rubber O-ring mounted on the mating surface of the outer wheel half of a split type wheel?","idealAnswer":"To prevent air leakage from the tubeless tire used with this wheel.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-009","question":"What are three types of aircraft brake systems?","idealAnswer":"The independent, the power boosted, and the power control type.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-010","question":"What is the purpose of a shuttle valve in a power control system?","idealAnswer":"Each brake actuating line in a power brake system incorporates a shuttle valve for the purpose of isolating the emergency brake system from the normal brake system. When emergency brake actuating pressure enters the shuttle valve, the shuttle is systematically moved to the opposite end of the valve. This closes off the normal hydraulic brake system actuating line and allows the emergency fluid to actuate the brakes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-011","question":"What is the purpose of the main landing gear Torque link?","idealAnswer":"Keep the landing gear in alignment.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-012","question":"What is used to prevent a nose gear from being retracted with the wheel out of the center position?","idealAnswer":"Centering devices, such as an internal centering cam or an external track that straighten the nose wheel before it enters the wheel well.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-013","question":"What is used in a split wheel assembly to keep the air from leaking aut hetween the wheel halves?","idealAnswer":"An O-ring.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-014","question":"What is the purpose of the fusible plug in some aircraft wheels?","idealAnswer":"It is designed to melt at a specific temperature and relieve air pressure to prevent the tire from blowing out or damaging the wheel.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-015","question":"How should aircraft tires be stored?","idealAnswer":"They should be stored in a cool, dry place, away from chemicals, oil, or sunlight.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-016","question":"What is the most important maintenance consideration for aircraft tires?","idealAnswer":"Ensuring they are properly inflated.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-017","question":"What safety procedure should be followed when inflating tires in general?","idealAnswer":"They should be inflated inside of a cage whenever possible.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-018","question":"What is the purpose of the anti-skid system?","idealAnswer":"To bring the airplane to a stop without skidding by providing the e most effective braking power under all types of runway conditions.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-019","question":"What task should be accomplished if the aircraft brakes are spongy?","idealAnswer":"The brakes need to be bled.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-020","question":"What are two methods of bleeding brakes?","idealAnswer":"The gravity method and the pressure method.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-021","question":"When inspecting brakes, what do you look for?","idealAnswer":"ry 1. Check for leaks while the system is pressurized. e 2. Check flex hoses for swelling, cracking, or soft spots. 3. Check brake pedals for spongy actuation. (This is an indication of air in the system.) id 4. Check brake pedals for excessive travel. (This indicates worn linings.) '","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-022","question":"What must you do before disassembling an oleo strut?","idealAnswer":"Discharge the pressure.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-K-023","question":"Where can you find instructions for jacking one wheel of an aircraft to change a tire?","idealAnswer":"The aircraft maintenance manual.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-l","letter":"L","title":"L. Hydraulic and Pneumatic Power Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-L-001","question":"What are the three principal categories of hydraulic fluids?","idealAnswer":"1. Mineral (5606), 2. Polyalphaolefin (poly alpha olefin) (83282), 3. Phosphate ester (Skydrol)","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-002","question":"What is the color of mineral based hydraulic fluid?","idealAnswer":"Red.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-003","question":"How do you determine which type of hydraulic fluid to use in a specific system?","idealAnswer":"The type of fluid is specified in the aircraft maintenance manual, or on the instruction plate attached to the reservoir on the unit being serviced.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-004","question":"What will happen to the seals in a vegetable base hydraulic system if it is serviced with mineral base or phosphate ester base fluids?","idealAnswer":"The seals will swell, break down, and block the system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-005","question":"When installing a chevron seal what must be considered?","idealAnswer":"A chevron seal is directional and must be installed with pressure going toward the open side of the seal.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-006","question":"When hydraulic lines have been disconnected, what precautions should be taken to prevent contamination of the system?","idealAnswer":"All hydraulic lines and fittings should be capped or plugged immediately after disconnecting.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-007","question":"What happens to the hydraulic fluid flow if the filter element becomes clogged with foreign matter?","idealAnswer":"There is a bypass valve which routes the hydraulic fluid directly from the inlet port to the outlet port.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-008","question":"What is a micronic filter?","idealAnswer":"A filter with a paper element.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-009","question":"Name the filters used in aircraft pneumatic systems?","idealAnswer":"Paper pleated and screen type.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-010","question":"Why are some hydraulic reservoirs pressurized?","idealAnswer":"To ensure a positive flow of fluid to the engine driven pump at high altitudes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-011","question":"What methods are used to pressurize hydraulic reservoirs?","idealAnswer":"Some systems use a variable displacement hydraulic pump, engine bleed air, or hydraulic system pressure acting on a small piston inside the reservoir. Another method is an aspirator or Venturi.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-012","question":"What are the two types of accumulator housings?","idealAnswer":"Spherical, and cylindrical.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-013","question":"What is the purpose of a pressure rellef valve in a hydraulic system?","idealAnswer":"It is to limit the amount of pressure In the system. It is, in effect, a system safety valve.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-014","question":"What is the purpose of an unloading valve in a closed hydraulic system?","idealAnswer":"The unloading valve is designed to provide a low-pressure path for the fluid to flow from the pump back to the reservoir when the system has no flow requirements. The unloading vaive also acts as a pressure regulator.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-015","question":"What is the function of a wing flap overload valve?","idealAnswer":"The purpose of the valve is to prevent possible structural damage to the flaps or the flap system that might result from the flaps being lowered at excessive speeds.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-016","question":"What is the purpose of the shear-able section in a hydraulic pump?","idealAnswer":"In the event the pump was to seize, the shear section will disconnect the pump from the engine, allowing the engine to continue to operate.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-017","question":"What are two different types of hydraulic pumps?","idealAnswer":"There are hand pumps and power pumps. Pumps can also be classified as positive displacement, * or nonpositive displacement.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-018","question":"What pump is most suitable for low-pressure pneumatic systems?","idealAnswer":"A vane-type pump.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-019","question":"What protects a direct-pressure type hydraulic pressure gauge from pressure surges?","idealAnswer":"' A dampening device in the form of a very small restrictor is installed in the line leading to the gauge, ' or at the inlet to the gauge itself.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-020","question":"What are pneumatic systems used for in aircraft?","idealAnswer":". Pneumatic systems are sometimes used to operate brakes, open and close doors, starting, pump ry driving, and operating emergency devices. 1 22. What happens to the air after a pneumatic system is operated? The air is dumped overboard.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-021","question":"Why should a pneumatic system be purged periodically?","idealAnswer":"To remove contamination, moisture, or oil from the components and lines.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-022","question":"What is the purpose of the relief valve in a pneumatic system?","idealAnswer":"The relief valve protects the system against over-pressurization (thermal expansion) by venting excess pressure to the atmosphere.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-023","question":"What is the purpose of an accumulator?","idealAnswer":"Accumulators are used to prevent hydraulic system shock when under high load demand conditions.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-L-024","question":"What would cause a hydraulic filter to go into full bypass mode allowing unfiltered fluid into the system?","idealAnswer":"A clogged filter.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-m","letter":"M","title":"M. Cabin Atmosphere Control Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-M-001","question":"What is the principal control element in a cabin pressurization system?","idealAnswer":"The cabin outflow valve.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-002","question":"What is the purpose of the negative pressure relief valve in the pressurization system?","idealAnswer":"The negative pressure relief valve prevents accidentally obtaining a cabin altitude which is higher than the altitude of the aircraft.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-003","question":"What control changes the position of the outflow valve?","idealAnswer":"The pressurization controller, located in the cockpit is used to set the cabin air pressure to a desired altitude, normally between six and eight thousand feet.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-004","question":"What are the two groups of independent cabin compressors?","idealAnswer":"They are positive displacement, and centrifugal compressors.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-005","question":"What method is used by turbine powered aircraft for pressurization?","idealAnswer":"Bleed air from the engine compressor is used for pressurization.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-006","question":"What limits the degree of cabin pressurization?","idealAnswer":"The structural design features of the fuselage, and by the capacity of the superchargers to maintain a constant volume of airflow to the fuselage.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-007","question":"Where does the warm air for cabin heating come from in most small reciprocating engine aircraft?","idealAnswer":"From the shroud around the engine muffler.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-008","question":"What are three methods of supplying heat to the conditioned air when the \"heat of compression\" is not sufficient for this purpose?","idealAnswer":"Fuel burning combustion heaters, electric heaters, and exhaust gas air-to-air heat exchangers.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-009","question":"What is the function of ventilating air ina combustion heater?","idealAnswer":"Ventilation air is used to carry the heat to the places where it is needed.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-010","question":"Where does the fuel used in an aircraft combustion heater come from?","idealAnswer":"The aircraft fuel tanks.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-011","question":"What controls the temperature in an aircraft cabin that is heated with a combustion heater?","idealAnswer":"A thermostat","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-012","question":"What are the sources for ventilation air ina combustion heater?","idealAnswer":"For air circulation and heater operation on the ground a blower is used. Inflight, ram-air inlets, or cabin compressors on pressurized aircraft.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-013","question":"What are the components of an air cycle cooling system?","idealAnswer":"An expansion turbine (cooling system), an air-to-air heat exchanger, and various valves which control airflow through the system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-014","question":"What causes a temperature drop in an air cycle cooling system?","idealAnswer":"As the compressed air passes through the expansion turbine, it performs the work of turning the turbine and undergoes a pressure and temperature drop.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-015","question":"What are the components in a vapor cycle (Freon) cooling system?","idealAnswer":"The compressor, condenser, receiver/dryer, expansion valve, and evaporator. a Se","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-016","question":"How do you determine the liquid level in a vapor cycle cooling system?","idealAnswer":"Operate the system for about 5 minutes to reach a stabilized condition, and then observe the flow of Freon through the sight glass. A steady flow indicates sufficient charge. If the Freon is low, bubbles will appear in the sight glass.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-017","question":"How is the vapor-cycle cooling system compressor lubricated?","idealAnswer":"Specialized refrigerant oil.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-018","question":"What are the components of a continuous flow oxygen system?","idealAnswer":"Oxygen will flow continuously from a high-pressure cylinder, through a high-pressure line to the pressure regulation reducing valve. The oxygen will then flow through a calibrated orifice in the outlet, controlling the amount of oxygen delivered to the rebreathing mask.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-019","question":"What must be done if an oxygen system has been depleted and not recharged within two hours?","idealAnswer":"It must be purged with dry nitrogen or dry air and oxygen.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-020","question":"What safety considerations must be observed when filling oxygen cylinders?","idealAnswer":"Oxygen cylinders should be filled in an oil free environment. The mechanic who fills the cylinders should not have any petroleum products on their clothes, hands, or in their hair.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-021","question":"What is the main chemical agent inside of an oxygen generator?","idealAnswer":"Sodium chlorate is the main chemical agent used in aviation oxygen generators. The chemical is ‘ ignited by a spark-emitting cap, and when it burns, 02 is generated. These systems are lighter and less complex than oxygen storage-based systems. The main drawback of oxygen generators is that once the burning of the sodium chlorate begins, it cannot be stopped until the chemical is consumed.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-022","question":"How are high- and low-pressure oxygen cylinders identified?","idealAnswer":"High pressure (1800-1850 psi) oxygen cylinders are green in color and have the words \"Aviator's Breathing Oxygen\" stenciled lengthwise in white, one-inch letters. Low pressure (400-425 psi) cylinders are painted light yellow.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-023","question":"What are the three ways supplemental oxygen can be carried in an aircraft?","idealAnswer":"As liquid oxygen, a high-pressure gas, or a chemical candle.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-024","question":"What is an acceptable lubricant for installing oxygen system fittings?","idealAnswer":"Teflon, or water-based lubricants.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-025","question":"What type or grade of oxygen must be used in aircraft?","idealAnswer":"Aviators-grade breathing oxygen.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-M-026","question":"What is a roots blower?","idealAnswer":"A type of engine driven compressor. )","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-n","letter":"N","title":"N. Aircraft Instrument Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-N-001","question":"What three flight instruments are normally operated from the pitot-static system?","idealAnswer":"The airspeed indicator is connected to the pitot and static pressure sources. The altimeter and vertical speed indicator are connected to the static pressure source only.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-002","question":"What instrument indications will result if the static pressure line becomes disconnected inside the cabin of a pressurized aircraft?","idealAnswer":"The altimeter and airspeed indications will both be lower than normal, and the vertical speed indicator will indicate a momentary decent then return to zero.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-003","question":"Who is authorized to perform tests to calibrate an altimeter?","idealAnswer":"The manufacturer, or a certified repair station.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-004","question":"What is required after replacement of components connected to the pitot-static system?","idealAnswer":"A leak test is required.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-005","question":"In wet-type vacuum pumps, what are the vanes made from?","idealAnswer":"Steel.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-006","question":"How are vanes of a wet-type pump lubricated?","idealAnswer":"Engine oil.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-007","question":"What are the sources of power to gyroscopic instrument operation?","idealAnswer":"Gyroscopic instruments can be operated either by vacuum, electricity, or air pressure.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-008","question":"What causes an electric driven gyro rotor to tilt when the aircraft is turned?","idealAnswer":"Gyroscopic precession.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-009","question":"What is used as a guide for range marking of aircraft instruments?","idealAnswer":"The Aircraft Specifications, or Type Certificate Data Sheets and the specific aircraft maintenance manual or flight manual.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-010","question":"Where are instrument range markings placed?","idealAnswer":"They are applied to the outer edge of the cover glass or over the calibrations on the dial face of the instrument.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-011","question":"What other type of mark is required when range markings are applied to the glass instrument cover?","idealAnswer":"An index mark, which is a white line extending from the glass cover onto the instrument case to indicate slippage of the glass. Glass slippage would cause the range markings to be in error.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-012","question":"What is a synchro-type remote indicating system?","idealAnswer":"These systems can be used to show movement and position of landing gear, wing flaps, oil cooler doors, or other movable parts of the aircraft. A synchro system is an electrical system used for transmitting information from one point to another. The three most common synchro systems are the Autosyn, the Selsyn, and the Magnesyn.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-013","question":"What type of fuel quantity system is an electric fuel measuring device that accurately determines the density | weight of the fuel in the tanks of an aircraft?","idealAnswer":"The capacitor fuel type quantity system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-014","question":"What is meant by \"swinging a compass\"?","idealAnswer":"It is the adjustment of the North-South, and East-West magnets to reduce deviation, which are magnetic influences of the aircraft structure and electrical systems. oO v.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-015","question":"When Inspecting a magnetic compass, the bow! should be filled with liquid and should not contain any ron bubbles or discolorations, What is the purpose of the liquid In the bowl?","idealAnswer":"It Is to dampen the oscillations of the float.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-016","question":"What is the fluid used ina magnetic compass?","idealAnswer":"A clear fluld such a kerosene. oe","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-017","question":"Where is the compass correction card placed?","idealAnswer":"Near the compass and in plain sight of the pilot. Pp =","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-018","question":"Which instrument is used to indicate the speed of the crankshaft of a reciprocating engine and the speed of \"» the main rotor assembly ina turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"A tachometer, a","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-019","question":"What type of indicating system is used to indicate turbine engine exhaust gas temperature (EGT)?","idealAnswer":"A thermocouple system. =","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-020","question":"What does the manifold pressure gauge indicate when the engine is not operating?","idealAnswer":"It indicates atmospheric Dy pressure,","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-021","question":"What is used to check a manifold pressure gauge for correct indication when the engine is not operating?","idealAnswer":"gaugt rp Either a barometer, or an altimeter after it has been set to zero. s ‘ ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-022","question":"What is the meaning of the yellow range on the airspeed indicator?","idealAnswer":"The yellow arc designates the cautionary D> range. = ;","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-023","question":"What does a Mach meter indicate?","idealAnswer":"The ratio of aircraft speed to the speed of sound at a particular altitude and temperature during anytime in flight. Ld)","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-N-024","question":"Why do you not try to remove a blockage in a pitot system?","idealAnswer":"To prevent further damage to the pitot system. = = = = = t) ca) a) ® ® @ en","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-o","letter":"O","title":"O. Communication and Navigation Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-O-001","question":"What is the most common communication system in use today?","idealAnswer":"It is the VHF (very high frequency) system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-002","question":"Which radio system is used for long range communication?","idealAnswer":"The HF (high frequency) system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-003","question":"What is a transceiver?","idealAnswer":"It is a self-contained transmitter and receiver which share the same power supply, antenna, and tuning device.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-004","question":"What type of conductor is used for transmitting information between antennas and receiver/transmitters?","idealAnswer":"Coaxial cable.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-005","question":"What are the components of a typical VOR (Very High Frequency Omni-range Navigation equipment) system?","idealAnswer":"A receiver, visual indicator, dipole antenna, and power supply.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-006","question":"What are the basic components of an autopilot system?","idealAnswer":"The gyros, servos, and amplifier.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-007","question":"What are the sensing elements of an autopilot system?","idealAnswer":"The directional gyro, turn-and-bank gyro, attitude gyro, and altitude control are the sensing elements. These units' sense aircraft pitch, roll, and yaw to automatically generate signals to keep these movements under control.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-008","question":"What are the output elements of an autopilot system?","idealAnswer":"The output elements of an autopilot system are the servos which actuate the control surfaces.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-009","question":"What are three types of servos used in autopilot system?","idealAnswer":"Hydraulic, pneumatic, and electric.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-010","question":"What is the purpose of a bonding jumper/wire on a radio shock mount?","idealAnswer":"To provide a low-impedance ground return and minimize radio interference from static electricity.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-011","question":"What is the frequency of an emergency locator transmitter (ELT)?","idealAnswer":"It is 121.5, 243.0, and 406.0 MHz (121.5 times 2 equals 243)","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-012","question":"How can you determine when the batteries of an ELT should be replaced?","idealAnswer":"The useful life of the batteries is determined by the battery manufacturer, and the battery replacement date must be marked on the outside of the transmitter. Time is computed from the date of manufacture of the battery.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-013","question":"Describe the proper method of routing coaxial cable from the antenna to the receiver. When installing coaxial cables, secure them firmly along their entire length at intervals of approximately two feet. To assure optimum operation, coaxial cables should not be routed or tied to other wire bundles and be as short as possible. When bending coaxial cable, be sure that the bend is at least 10 times the size of the cable diameter. 3 16. Where is the preferred location for mounting a VOR antenna on the small aircraft?","idealAnswer":"The VOR antenna is \"V\" shaped. The preferred location is on top of the forward part of the cabin . with the apex of the \"V\" toward the front. An acceptable alternate location is on top of the vertical ~ stabilizer.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-014","question":"Where should antennas be located?","idealAnswer":"They should be mounted on the lower surface of the aircraft ina position i that will not be blanketed by the wings when the aircraft is banked.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-015","question":"Why are doublers required to be installed under aircraft skin when an antenna is mounted on the aircraft skin?","idealAnswer":"To prevent cracking in the skin.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-016","question":"What are marker beacons?","idealAnswer":"It is part of the ILS system and is used to indicate the aircraft position on the approach to the runway.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-017","question":"What is the indication of the middle marker beacon?","idealAnswer":"It is an amber light and an audible tone.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-018","question":"What is an ADF?","idealAnswer":"An automatic direction finder.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-019","question":"What does DME stand for?","idealAnswer":"Distance measuring equipment.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-O-020","question":"What two parts make up the ILS system?","idealAnswer":"Localizer and glideslope. 24. Explain how to determine the pressure altitude to an airfield and hot to set it in the altimeter. You can listen to the ATC or tune in the Automatic Terminal Information Service (ATIS), and they will announce the barometric pressure altitude. You will then dial in that setting on the altimeter in the Kollsman window. Also, you can dial in the known field elevation and the altimeter will reflect the barometric pressure.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-p","letter":"P","title":"P. Aircraft Fuel Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-P-001","question":"What is the purpose of a fuel dump system?","idealAnswer":"It is an emergency system provided so that the flight crew can quickly bring the weight of the airplane down to the maximum landing weight if an emergency occurs during or shortly after takeoff. 2. Other than reducing weight, what other function can be accomplished with a fuel jettisoning system? Fuel can be dumped from the heavy wing to maintain lateral stability. A fuel dump system is required for all transport airplanes if the maximum takeoff weight is more than 105% of the maximum landing weight. The airplane must be free of fire hazards during dumping, and fuel must dump free and clear of the airplane.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-002","question":"What are a few types of fuel cells?","idealAnswer":"Bladder-type fuel cells, integral fuel cells, integral fuel tanks, and welded or riveted fuel tanks.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-003","question":"How is the weight of a bladder-type cell supported?","idealAnswer":"The bladder-type cell depends on the structure of the cavity in which it sits to support the weight of the fuel within it and maintains the shape of a cell. They are made of either rubber or nylon impregnated with neoprene.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-004","question":"What is \"wet-wing\" construction?","idealAnswer":"Wet wing construction is when the fuel cells are built into the wings of the aircraft structure using the skin to contain the fuel. They are known as integral fuel cells.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-005","question":"What markings must be placed near the fuel filler opening on reciprocating engine aircraft?","idealAnswer":"The word \"Avgas\" and the minimum fuel grade.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-006","question":"What are the advantages of a single point pressure fueling system and what is it sometimes called?","idealAnswer":"Pressure fueling, sometimes referred to as single-point or underwing fueling, and greatly reduces the time required to service large aircraft. \\t also eliminates aircraft skin damage from hoses and hose nozzles and reduces the chance of fuel contamination.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-007","question":"After fueling an aircraft, why is it important for a technician to wait at least 30 minutes prior to taking a fuel sample?","idealAnswer":"Fueling aircraft causes turbulence in the tanks, which results in contaminants such as water and solid particles to mix with the fuel. It is important for the technician to allow enough time for water and contamination to settle into the sumps prior to pulling a fuel sample.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-008","question":"Why should the fuel sumps of aircraft be drained before the first flight of the day?","idealAnswer":"To remove any water that has condensed in the fuel and collected in the sump.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-009","question":"What does water look like when in fuel that has been drained from the sumps?","idealAnswer":"It looks like clear balls of jelly.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-010","question":"What is the purpose of a fuel temperature indicator on a turbine engine?","idealAnswer":"It is a means for checking the temperature of the fuel in the tanks at the engine to determine when there may be a danger of ice crystals forming in the fuel. ee","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-011","question":"What is the purpose of warning lights in connection with fuel system?","idealAnswer":"\" Warning lights may be used to indicate when the fuel pressure is too low, or to indicate when the fuel quantity in a tank is below a certain amount. ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-012","question":"Where are electrically operated valve-in-transit indicator lights used?","idealAnswer":"~ On large multiengine aircraft, each of the fuel cross feed and line valves may be provided with valve in- * transit indicator lights. The light is on only during the time the valve is in motion and is off when movement is complete.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-013","question":"Why do fuel selectors have detents?","idealAnswer":"To \"lock\" the switch into the selected position and so the operator can feel when the switch is in a specific position.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-014","question":"What are four general types of fuel quantity gauges?","idealAnswer":"\" The four types are sight glass (visual), mechanical (linkage), electrical (transmitter, receiver), and electronic (totalizer).","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-015","question":"Which type of fuel quantity gauge is more accurate than the other types?","idealAnswer":"The electronic-capacitance type because it measures by weight.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-016","question":"Describe the sensor used in an electronic type fuel quantity indicating system?","idealAnswer":"It is a tubular capacitor that extends across the tank from top to bottom.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-017","question":"What is a drip gauge?","idealAnswer":"= An under-wing bayonet type fuel gauge that consists of a calibrated hollow drip tube that is drawn . out from the lower wing surface. When the top of the tube reaches the level of the fuel, the tube will begin to drip indicating fuel level. 7 19. What is a cross-feed system? An interconnected fuel system designed so that fuel can be fed from any tank to any engine.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-018","question":"What is the purpose of the baffles in a fuel tank?","idealAnswer":"To prevent the fuel from surging because of changes in attitude of the aircraft. .","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-019","question":"How can a fuel tank be checked for leaks after a repair?","idealAnswer":"By a pressure check using air pressure not over one-half psi with liquid soap or bubble solution to = identify the leak. .","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-020","question":"What is another name for a pressure type refueling system?","idealAnswer":"Single point refueling system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-P-021","question":"What is the purpose of a jettisoning system on a small aircraft?","idealAnswer":"To obtain a safe landing weight. » a Cad cd td Pp","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-q","letter":"Q","title":"Q. Aircraft Electrical Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-001","question":"What are the two principles on which circuit breakers operate?","idealAnswer":"Thermal and magnetic.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-002","question":"What tool is used to measure the size of unmarked electrical wire?","idealAnswer":"A wire gauge can be used to measure wires ranging in size from number zero to number 36.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-003","question":"What are the colors of aircraft position lights and where are they mounted?","idealAnswer":"Agreen light is mounted on the right wingtip, a red light on the left wingtip, and a white light mounted in a position where it is visible from the rear of the aircraft.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-004","question":"What are the factors to be considered when selecting wire size for electrical power?","idealAnswer":"One factor is the allowable power loss in the line. A second factor is the permissible voltage drop in the line, and a third factor is the current-carrying ability of the conductor. G. When is a circuit breaker or fuse designed lo open the circuit? A circuit breaker or fuse should open the circuit before the conductor emits smoke. To accomplish this, the current characteristics of the protective devise must fall below that of the associated conductor.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-005","question":"What type of breakers are not acceptable for use in aircraft?","idealAnswer":"Auto-reset breakers.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-006","question":"What is a slow-blow fuse?","idealAnswer":"A fuse that compensates for surges and high inrush current by not blowing for several milliseconds.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-007","question":"What are the three most common faults that occur in an electrical circuit?","idealAnswer":"3 They are open circuits, in which wires are broken, shorted circuits, in which ground leads : cause current to be returned by shortcuts to the source of power; and _in circuits causing lights to burn dimly and relays to chatter.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-008","question":"What size electrical conduit should be used for a specific cable bundle?","idealAnswer":"; To allow for ease of maintenance and possible future circuit expansion, the conduit should have an i inside diameter that is 25% larger than the maximum diameter of the conductor bundle.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-009","question":"What color insulator on a solderless connector is proper for a 10-gauge wire?","idealAnswer":"Yellow.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-010","question":"How should electrical switches typically be oriented in the on position?","idealAnswer":"Up or forward.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-011","question":"When should electrical switches be de-rated from their normal current rating?","idealAnswer":"For high rush-in circuits such as circuits containing incandescent lamps (landing lights), and inductive circuits that have magnetic energy stored in solenoid coils, or relays, and for DC motor circuits, which draw several times their rated current during starting.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-012","question":"When do DC electric motors draw several times their rated current?","idealAnswer":"During starting. A BADR LEIS AD AT ed BIEN SS","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-013","question":"What would cause an AC electrical motor to run too fast?","idealAnswer":"An excessive supply of voltage, or the motor field windings shorted.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-014","question":"What would cause an AC electrical motor to run too slow?","idealAnswer":"Low applied voltage, defective wiring, or no lubrication.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-015","question":"How is the output voltage of an alternator controlled?","idealAnswer":"By using a voltage regulator to control the exciter field current and thus regulate the exciter output voltage applied to the alternator field.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-016","question":"What should be checked before any aircraft electrical load is increased?","idealAnswer":"wes The associated wires, conductors, and circuit protection devices should be checked to determine that the new load will not exceed the rated limits of these units.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-017","question":"What is the main advantage of using AC for aircraft electrical power systems?","idealAnswer":"The principal advantage is that the voltage of AC power systems is easily changed using transformers. Therefore, you can transmit power at high voltage and low current, thus reducing the ball size and weight of the wiring in the circuit. Lad","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-018","question":"In AC power systems, how is DC obtained for battery charging?","idealAnswer":"The alternating current is changed to direct current using rectifiers. -","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-019","question":"What warns pilots of overheating batteries?","idealAnswer":"A thermal sensor in the battery and a light in the cockpit.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-020","question":"How long should bonding jumper wires be made?","idealAnswer":"Large enough to handle all the return current flow and as short L as possible to prevent an increase in resistance. a","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-021","question":"What are the two items that wear on a starter generator?","idealAnswer":"Commutator and the brushes.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-022","question":"What powers the ELT?","idealAnswer":"The ELT battery.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-Q-023","question":"What is the purpose of a static wick?","idealAnswer":"It dissipates static energy from the aircraft to avoid damaging electrical Lad components. @ @ @ a a 2a a @ a E 65 Aa (5G eh « fd of","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-r","letter":"R","title":"R. Position and Warning Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-R-001","question":"What is the purpose of the anti-skid system?","idealAnswer":"To bring the airplane to a stop without skidding by providing the we most effective braking power under all types uf runway conditions. oe","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-002","question":"What does the anti-skid warning light indicate?","idealAnswer":"The anti-skid warning light indicates that the system is turned off € or there is a system failure. #","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-003","question":"What type ot warning devices are usually provided for retractable landing gear?","idealAnswer":"A red light and aural device. cd A. When does the landing gear warning horn operate in a landing gear warning system? oe When the throttles are retarded, and the landing gear is in any position other than down and locked. o","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-004","question":"What type of position indicators are normally used to indicate that the landing gear is down and locked?","idealAnswer":"Ld Normally there is a green light for each gear. ~","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-005","question":"Which positions of retractable landing gear normally have a position indicator?","idealAnswer":"The up and locked position, down i and locked position, and in transit. baad","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-006","question":"What is a squat, or weight on wheels switch, and where is it located?","idealAnswer":"It is a switch that detects if the aircraft is on e the ground. They will be in the torsion links of the main landing gear and prevents accidental retraction of the gear while the aircraft is on the ground. e , ' had","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-007","question":"What device is used as a sensor to detect the condition of a retractable landing gear?","idealAnswer":"rn Amicroswitch. (Commonly called squat switch or weight on wheels switch.) ; Log","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-008","question":"When should you check landing gear switches, lights, warning horn, or buzzer for proper operation?","idealAnswer":"te During a landing gear retraction check or swinging the landing gear. e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-009","question":"How does a takeoff warning horn differ from a landing gear warning horn?","idealAnswer":"¢ The takeoff horn is intermittent, whereas the landing gear warning is continuous. @","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-010","question":"What items may cause a takeoff warning horn to operate in a typical transport jet aircraft?","idealAnswer":"e If the throttles are advanced and any of the following conditions exist, the takeoff warning horn will e sound. @ 1. Speed brakes not down. 3. Flaps not in takeoff range. ?. Auxiliary power exhaust door open. 4. Stabilizer not in the takeoff setting. e e","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-011","question":"What is the master caution light?","idealAnswer":"It is a light that alerts the pilot that there is an abnormal or change in condition a with one of the aircraft systems. «","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-012","question":"What is used to indicate the position of wing flaps?","idealAnswer":"A resistance-type remote indicating system. te","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-R-013","question":"What are the positions of aircraft navigation lights?","idealAnswer":"Red left wing _/ Green right wing / White tail am Remember \"REDS NOT RIGHT\" és » e a a","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-s","letter":"S","title":"S. Ice and Rain Control Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-S-001","question":"Describe the operating principle of inflatable deicer boots. Pneumatic deicing systems use rubber inflatable boots attached to the leading edge of the wings and stabilizers. During operation, the tubes are inflated with pressurized air and deflated in an alternating cycle. This inflation and deflation causes the ice to break off and it is carried away by the air stream. Both wings inflate and deflate at the same time. 7 2. Why is critical to prevent the formation of ice on aircraft wings and control surfaces?","idealAnswer":"Ice formation distorts the shape of the wing and air flowing over it, reducing aerodynamic lift. Also, ice weighs the aircraft down, adding stress to the airframe.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-002","question":"What are two methods of inflating pneumatic deicer boots?","idealAnswer":"Pressurized air is provided by an engine driven air pump (vacuum pump), or by bleed air from the - turbine engine compressor.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-003","question":"What methods are used to attach deicer boots to the leading edges of wing and tail surfaces?","idealAnswer":"They are attached by bonding to the surface with cement, fairing strips and screws, or a combination of both methods.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-004","question":"What is the purpose of an air-oil separator in a pneumatic deicing system?","idealAnswer":". The purpose of removing the oil from the air of the deicing system is to prevent deterioration of the rubber deicing boots, caused by contact with the oil.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-005","question":"What is used to clean deicer boots?","idealAnswer":"A mild soap and water solution.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-006","question":"What must be done before you can make a cold patch repair to a deicer boot?","idealAnswer":"* Clean, rough the surface of the boot and patch, glue and attach.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-007","question":"What component of a pneumatic deicer system normally allows suction to be supplied to the boots to hold them down in flight?","idealAnswer":"The solenoid distributor valve.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-008","question":"Describe the operating principle for thermal anti-icing systems?","idealAnswer":"Thermal anti-icing systems are used primarily for the purpose of preventing ice from forming on the leading edges. However, they are designed to also deice the leading edges by supplying much hotter than normal air for short periods on a cyclic system. The airfoils that are to be protected are usually provided with closely spaced double skin. The hot air is passed through the space between skins, and the heat is sufficient to prevent formation of ice or to melt any ice next to the skin. The heated air is supplied continuously if the anti-icing system is turned on. Once used, this heated air is vented overboard through a hole in the leading edge.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-009","question":"How can you determine if the heater in a pitot is working?","idealAnswer":"Turn it on and feel for heat coming from it.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-010","question":"What methods are used to supply heated air for thermal anti-icing systems?","idealAnswer":"Bleed air from the turbine engine compressor, engine exhaust heat exchangers, and ram air heated by a combustion heater.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-011","question":"How is overheating prevented in a thermal anti-icing system using bleed air?","idealAnswer":"A thermal switch will cause a shut-off valve to close and stop the flow of bleed air when the temperature of the leading edge reaches approximately 185°F. When the temperature drops, the valve opens and hot bleed air enters the wing once again.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-012","question":"What is the purpose of the de-ice boot electrical timers?","idealAnswer":"To inflate the boots symmetrically. Or, in the case of an electrical system, to cycle each boot individually preventing an excessive load on the electrical system. 14. Ina thermal anti-icing system, what happens to the air after it has heated the leading edge of the wing? The air is exhausted to the atmosphere at the wingtip or at points where ice formation could be critical.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-013","question":"What problems are associated with electrically heated windshields?","idealAnswer":"Delamination, arcing, scratches, and discoloration. : 16.. What is windshield delamination? Delamination is the separation of the plies.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-014","question":"What does arcing usually indicate in an electrically heated windshield?","idealAnswer":"Arcing usually indicates that there is a ) breakdown in the conductive coating.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-015","question":"What type of power is used to operate windshield wiper systems?","idealAnswer":"Electrical or hydraulic power.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-016","question":"What are common problems encountered with windshield wipers?","idealAnswer":"One problem is the tendency of the acrodynamic forces to reduce the wiper blade loading pressure an the windshield which causes ineffective wiping or streaking. Another problem is achieving fast enough wiper oscillation.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-017","question":"How does a pneumatic rain removal system work?","idealAnswer":"This method uses high air pressure. The high temperature engine compressor bleed air is blown across the windshields, this air blast forms a barrier that prevents raindrops from striking the windshield surface.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-S-018","question":"Why should your windshield wipers not be operated when the windshield wipers are dry?","idealAnswer":"This can scratch the glass.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]},{"source":"baker-2022","section":"airframe","key":"baker-airframe-t","letter":"T","title":"T. Fire Protection Systems","questions":[{"id":"BAKER-A-T-001","question":"In what area of an aircraft would you find carbon monoxide detectors?","idealAnswer":"CO detectors are commonly used to check for the presence of carbon monoxide gas in aircraft cabins and cockpits. They are placed where they are easily visible.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-002","question":"What color does a CO detector change to when carbon monoxide fumes are present?","idealAnswer":"Areliable CO detector is an indicator tube or packet which contains yellow silica that is impregnated with a silicomolybdate compound. When air containing carbon monoxide is drawn through the tube . or packet, the silica gel turns a shade of green. Other types change from a tan color to gray or black.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-003","question":"What are two types of smoke detectors?","idealAnswer":"Photoelectric and visual.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-004","question":"Where are smoke detectors used in the aircraft?","idealAnswer":"They are commonly used in cargo and baggage compartments, a and the lavatory.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-005","question":"How are thermal switches wired in thermal switch fire detection systems?","idealAnswer":"The switches are wired in parallel with each other, but in series with the warning lights.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-006","question":"How does a thermocouple fire detection system differ from the thermal switch system?","idealAnswer":"A thermocouple depends upon the rate of temperature rise and will not give a warning when the @ engine overheats slowly or a short circuit develops. -","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-007","question":"What are two types of fire extinguishing systems?","idealAnswer":"The high rate of discharge (HRD) system uses Halon as an agent - and is effective on all fire classes. Conventional fire systems use carbon dioxide and are effective on class B and C P fires.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-008","question":"What type extinguishing agent is usually found in a HRD system?","idealAnswer":"The extinguishing agent is usually one of the halogenated hydrocarbons (Halons) boosted by high pressure dry nitrogen. Halon 1301 is the most common and e sometimes referred to as Freon 13.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-009","question":"What type extinguishing agent is usually associated with a conventional type extinguishing system?","idealAnswer":"a This system usually contains CO2 but may use any other adequate agent.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-010","question":"What is the purpose of the yellow disk in a CO2 fire extinguishing system?","idealAnswer":"a It is the system discharge indicator disk, and it indicates which bank of bottles has been emptied through normal discharge.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-011","question":"How can you determine the charge of a CO2 extinguisher?","idealAnswer":"By weighing it. 14. Ina turbine engine Freon (Halon) fire extinguishing system, how are bottles discharged? By an explosive discharge cartridge (Squib) which is detonated electrically, breaking a frangible disk.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-012","question":"How is a Freon fire extinguishing system protected from temperature rises more than set limits?","idealAnswer":"The system is equipped with a thermal fuse that will melt at high temperature conditions and will blow the red discharge indicator disk.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-013","question":"How does ambient temperature affect the pressure reading on a fire extinguisher?","idealAnswer":"Low temperatures will decrease the absolute pressure, and high temperatures increase the absolute pressure.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-014","question":"What type of extinguisher is recommended for brake fires?","idealAnswer":"A dry-powder extinguisher.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-015","question":"How can you determine the service life of a fire extinguisher discharge cartridge?","idealAnswer":"By the manufacturer's date stamped on the face of the cartridge. The service life of the cartridge is usually recommended in terms of hours below a predetermined temperature.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-016","question":"What tyne of hand-held fire extinguishers are available for extinguishing interior aircraft fires?","idealAnswer":"The three most common type hand fire extinguishers for a Class A fire are water, carbon dioxide (C02), and dry chemical extinguishing agents.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-017","question":"What type of fire detection system gives a more complete coverage of a fire hazard?","idealAnswer":"Acontinuous-loop detector system.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-018","question":"How does a photoelectric smoke detector work?","idealAnswer":"The smoke particulates block the photoelectric receptor from sensing light.","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"},{"id":"BAKER-A-T-019","question":"How would you determine when the next hydrostatic test is due on a portable fire bottle?","idealAnswer":"Look at the label for the DOT information to determine when the next test is due. It will be five years from the last test date. v bad e » Section IV - Powerplant Theory and wy ° Maintenance Oral Questions ®ection IV - Powerplant Theory and Maintenance (3 Subjects) iz ®eciprocating Engines SubjectA | 72 Surbine Engines Subject B 74 Engine Inspection SubjectC | 76 @FAA Reserved) Subjects D, E, F, G | N/A e ‘","source":"Baker School of Aeronautics Oral and Practical Study Guide (2022)"}]}];
